{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a5353fc44b8401ef", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tarXiv:2607.26607v1 [cs.SD] 29 Jul 2026\n2\t\n3\tFew-Shot Open-Set Audio Classification via\n4\tTransductive Prototype Refinement and Class Logit\n5\tEnhancement\n6\tTianyan Deng\n7\t\n8\tYanxiong Li*\n9\t\n10\tSchool of Mathematics\n11\tSouth China University of Technology\n12\tGuangzhou, China\n13\t202330400581@mail.scut.edu.cn\n14\t\n15\tSchool of Electronic and Information Engineering\n16\tSouth China University of Technology\n17\tGuangzhou, China\n18\teeyxli@scut.edu.cn\n19\t\n20\tRui Gao\n21\t\n22\tJiahao Du\n23\t\n24\tSchool of Electronic and Information Engineering\n25\tSouth China University of Technology\n26\tGuangzhou, China\n27\t202330350401@mail.scut.edu.cn\n28\t\n29\tSchool of Electronic and Information Engineering\n30\tSouth China University of Technology\n31\tGuangzhou, China\n32\t202330350311@mail.scut.edu.cn\n33\t\n34\tAbstract—Few-shot Open-set audio classification requires classifying query samples from known classes with a few labeled\n35\tsupport samples while rejecting query samples from unknown\n36\tclasses. Transductive inference jointly observes the full unlabeled query set to improve prototype estimation, yet standard\n37\ttransductive updates do not distinguish known from unknown\n38\tquery samples, leaving prototypes vulnerable to open-set contamination. Drawing on latent-inlierness weighting and decoupled\n39\tscoring for unknown-class samples, we propose a two-phase\n40\ttransductive method operating over a frozen audio encoder. First,\n41\teach query sample is assigned a latent inlierness score that\n42\tdown-weights likely unknown-class samples, so that prototype\n43\trefinement is driven primarily by known-class evidence. The\n44\trefined prototypes are then directly optimized on a transductive\n45\tloss combining support cross-entropy, inlierness-weighted conditional entropy minimization, and inlierness-weighted marginal\n46\tentropy maximization, while open-set rejection uses a prioradaptive free-energy score that adjusts its threshold with the\n47\tprior proportion of unknown-class samples, decoupling detection\n48\tfrom classification. Experiments on three audio datasets show\n49\tour method achieves state-of-the-art results for few-shot openset audio classification under multiple experimental conditions.\n50\tCode is available at https://github.com/Gostyan/ROLE\n51\tIndex Terms—Few-shot open-set classification, transductive\n52\tinference, audio classification\n53\t\n54\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n55\tReal-world few-shot audio classification [1]–[4] rarely satisfies the closed-set assumption: query samples routinely include\n56\tsounds outside classes of support set. In the transductive\n57\tsetting, a model observes the full unlabeled query set jointly\n58\tduring inference, which improves episode-level prototype estimation [5], [6]. However, standard transductive updates treat\n59\tall query samples equally and cannot distinguish known-class\n60\tfrom unknown-class samples, so prototype estimation degrade\n61\t∗ Corresponding author: Yanxiong Li (eeyxli@scut.edu.cn).\n62\t\n63\tas the unknown-class proportion grows [7]–[9]. Existing transductive few-shot methods mainly target closed-set accuracy,\n64\twhile dedicated open-set methods do not fully exploit query\n65\tset structure. This tension is especially pronounced in audio,\n66\twhere environmental sounds span an open-ended category\n67\tspace and deployment conditions are highly variable.\n68\tFew-shot learning provides metric- and meta-learning foundations via Prototypical Networks [10], MAML [11], and\n69\tembedding baselines [12]. Few-shot open-set methods such as\n70\tOPP [13], MET [14], Glocal [15], TANE [16], and AISP [17]\n71\timprove inductive rejection through various prototype and\n72\tscoring strategies. Transductive methods, such as TIM [5] and\n73\tPrototype Rectification [6], improve closed-set class estimation but are not designed for open-set episodes. Contextual\n74\ttransductive FSOR [9] is the closest prior transductive open-set\n75\textension. OSLO [7] introduces latent-inlierness weighting for\n76\tprototype estimation and EOL [8] proposes decoupled outlierlogit scoring. Few-shot open-set keyword-spotting methods\n77\ttackle open-set challenges through multi-stage training [18],\n78\ton-device calibration [19], and cross-domain reprojection [20],\n79\tbut remain task-specific and do not address transductive prototype contamination for general audio classification.\n80\tWe propose Refinement-based Outlier-Logit Enhancement\n81\t(ROLE), a transductive algorithm for Few-shot Open-set Audio Classification (FOAC) operating on a frozen audio encoder.\n82\tOur contributions are as follows.\n83\tWe propose ROLE, a unified transductive inference procedure that couples latent-inlierness-guided prototype refinement with a prior-adaptive free-energy rejection score\n84\tin a single episode-level inference rule, requiring only a\n85\tfrozen pre-trained encoder with no episodic meta-training\n86\tor backbone fine-tuning.\n87\t• Evaluated on ESC-50, FSD-Kaggle2018, and Urban•\n88\t\n89\t\fSound8K, under 5-way 1-shot/5-shot and 20%/50%/80%\n90\toutlier ratios, our method achieves the highest AUROC\n91\tscores in 10 out of 18 evaluation settings, with macromean AUROC of 85.88/92.22 vs. 81.15/90.26 for the\n92\tstrongest baseline MET.\n93\tII. M ETHOD\n94\tA. Problem Formulation\n95\tWe consider a transductive N -way, K-shot open-set\n96\ts\n97\tepisode. The support set S = {(xsi , ysi )}N\n98\ti=1 (Ns = N K)\n99\tcontains labeled examples from N novel classes, and the\n100\tNq\n101\tmay contain both knownunlabeled query set Q = {xqj }j=1\n102\tclass samples (inliers) and unknown-class samples (outliers).\n103\tThe full query set is observed jointly during inference. The\n104\tgoal is to classify inlier query samples into one of N support\n105\tclasses and reject outliers with a scalar outlier score. We write\n106\t[N ] = {1, . . . , N } for the inlier label set.\n107\tROLE operates on a frozen pre-trained audio encoder\n108\tf (·) that maps each waveform to a normalized embedding\n109\tϕ = f (x)/∥f (x)∥. We write ϕsi and ϕqj for support and\n110\tquery embeddings respectively. ROLE optimizes only episodespecific parameters: class prototypes {µk }N\n111\tk=1 , re-initialized\n112\tper episode.\n113\tB. Design Principles\n114\tROLE is structured as a two-phase pipeline, and each\n115\tphase addresses a distinct challenge in transductive open-set\n116\tclassification.\n117\tPrototype bias from undifferentiated transduction. Standard transductive updates (e.g., TIM [5], Prototype Rectification [6]) incorporate all query evidence equally into prototype\n118\testimation. In open-set episodes, unknown-class query samples\n119\tinevitably participate in these updates, pulling prototypes away\n120\tfrom the true known-class centers and the bias grows with the\n121\tunknown-class ratio. Phase 1 mitigates this by introducing a\n122\tper-query latent inlierness score: query samples assessed as\n123\tunlikely to belong to any known class are automatically downweighted, so that primarily known-class evidence refines the\n124\tprototypes.\n125\tConflated classification and rejection under a single\n126\tsoftmax. A natural approach uses the softmax posterior over\n127\tknown classes for both classification and open-set rejection.\n128\tHowever, softmax normalizes only over known classes and\n129\tcannot express “this query sample belongs to none of them”:\n130\tan unknown-class query sample equidistant from all prototypes\n131\treceives a uniform but non-negligible posterior, appearing\n132\tsimilar to a genuinely ambiguous inlier. Phase 2 decouples\n133\tthe two objectives by deriving classification from a class-wise\n134\tsoftmax and open-set rejection from a sigmoid on the negative\n135\tlog-mean-exp of the same logits, which explicitly measures\n136\twhether total class evidence justifies considering the query\n137\tsample as known-class.\n138\tFigure 1 summarizes the resulting pipeline.\n139\t\n140\tC. Unified Transductive Inference\n141\tGiven a labeled support set and an unlabeled open-set query\n142\tset, ROLE first refines prototypes under latent inlierness and\n143\tthen further optimizes them on a transductive loss with a prioradaptive decoupled scoring objective.\n144\tPhase 1: Inlierness-guided prototype refinement. Phase 1\n145\trefines class prototypes using query-batch evidence while\n146\tpreventing unknown-class query samples from biasing the\n147\tresult. We write Norm(·) = (·)/∥ · ∥ for ℓ2 normaliza(0)\n148\ttion. Prototypes\n149\tare initialized from support means: µk =\n150\tP\n151\tNorm( i:ys =k ϕsi ). Each query sample j carries a latent\n152\ti\n153\t\n154\t(0)\n155\t\n156\tinlierness score ξj ∈ (0, 1) (initialized to ξj = 1), representing the model’s belief that query sample j belongs to\n157\tsome known class. Following the block-coordinate descent\n158\tof OSLO [7], three variables—soft assignments Z, inlierness\n159\tξ, and prototypes µ—are updated alternately for Tboot iterations with support assignments held fixed. At iteration t,\n160\t(t)\n161\t(t)\n162\tlet sjk = τ ⟨µk , ϕqj ⟩ denote the temperature-scaled cosine\n163\tsimilarity between query sample j and prototype k, and\n164\t(t)\n165\t(t)\n166\tπjk = Softmaxk∈[N ] (sjk ) the posterior probability that query\n167\tsample j belongs to class k.\n168\tSoft assignments are modulated by inlierness rather than\n169\tcommitted to hard pseudo-labels, which would propagate\n170\tassignment errors directly into prototypes:\n171\t\u0010\n172\t\u0011\n173\t(t+1)\n174\t(t) (t)\n175\tZjk\n176\t= Softmaxk∈[N ] ξj sjk\n177\t(1)\n178\tHere ξj acts as an inverse temperature: when ξj ≈ 1 the assignment peaks at the nearest prototype, when ξj ≈ 0 it flattens\n179\tto uniform, neutralizing the query sample’s contribution. The\n180\tinlierness score is then updated via the expected log-posterior\n181\tunder the soft assignment, measuring how well the knownclass structure explains query sample j:\n182\t!\n183\t1−b\n184\t1 X (t+1)\n185\t(t)\n186\t(t+1)\n187\tZjk log πjk + log\n188\t(2)\n189\tξj\n190\t= Sigmoid\n191\tλξ\n192\tb\n193\tk\n194\t\n195\twhere λξ > 0 controls sensitivity and b is the prior outlier\n196\tproportion of the episode. The bias term log 1−b\n197\tshifts ξj\n198\tb\n199\tdownward when b is large: at high outlier ratios, the default\n200\tσ(0) = 0.5 would place many outliers at an ambiguous midpoint, making them hard to suppress, the prior shift pushes the\n201\tthreshold so that only query samples with strong known-class\n202\tevidence retain high inlierness. Prototypes are re-estimated\n203\tby combining fixed support geometry with inlierness-filtered\n204\tquery evidence:\n205\t\n206\t\n207\tNq\n208\tX\n209\tX\n210\t(t+1)\n211\t(t+1) (t+1)\n212\tµk\n213\t= Norm\n214\tϕs i +\n215\tξj\n216\tZjk ϕqj  (3)\n217\ti: ysi =k\n218\t\n219\tj=1\n220\t\n221\tThe product ξj Zjk provides double gating: only query samples\n222\tthat are both likely inliers and confidently assigned to class k\n223\tinfluence µk , preventing unknown-class query samples from\n224\tshifting prototypes.\n225\tPhase 2: Transductive prototype optimization with decoupled scoring. Phase 2 directly optimizes the class pro-\n226\t\n227\t\fFig. 1. Overview of ROLE. A frozen audio encoder maps support and query audio to normalized embeddings. Phase 1 performs inlierness-guided prototype\n228\trefinement. Phase 2 centers episode geometry, optimizes prototypes with ξ-weighted transductive losses, and uses prior-adaptive free-energy rejection decoupled\n229\tfrom classification.\n230\t\n231\ttotypes {µk } on the episode-level transductive loss, further adapting the refined geometry to the observed query\n232\tdistribution. Because frozen encoders carry a shared bias\n233\tfrom pre-training,\n234\tP\n235\tPwe first