 b further lowers the free-energy threshold required\n323\tfor inlier acceptance as the outlier proportion b grows, so\n324\tthat the boundary between inlier and outlier decisions adapts\n325\tautomatically to episode difficulty.\n326\tThe prototypes {µ̃k } are optimized over Tcal gradient steps\n327\t\n328\tThe ξ-weighted marginal entropyPmaximization: since\n329\tp̂k log p̂k ≤ 0, minimizing +λma k p̂k log p̂k pushes p̂\n330\ttoward the uniform distribution, encouraging the prototype\n331\tgeometry to assign inlier query samples evenly across known\n332\tclasses and preventing collapse to a dominant class.\n333\tUnlike OSLO/EOL, ROLE uses inlierness-gated TIM-style\n334\toptimization before prior-adaptive free-energy scoring.\n335\tAlgorithm 1 summarizes the complete procedure of ROLE.\n336\tIII. E XPERIMENTS\n337\tA. Datasets\n338\tWe evaluate different methods on three audio datasets: ESC50 [21], FSD-Kaggle2018 [22] and UrbanSound8K [23]. The\n339\tencoder is pre-trained on AudioSet [24] and kept frozen.\n340\tNo dataset contributes a training split. All clips are used\n341\t\n342\t\fAlgorithm 1 ROLE\n343\tRequire: Support set S, query set Q, frozen encoder f\n344\tEnsure: Predicted label ŷj and outlier score ôj for each query\n345\tsample j\n346\t(0)\n347\t1: Encode and ℓ2 -normalise all embeddings; init µk from\n348\t(0)\n349\tsupport means, ξj =1\n350\t2: for t = 0, . . . , Tboot − 1 do\n351\t// Phase 1\n352\t(t)\n353\t(t)\n354\t(t)\n355\t(t)\n356\t3:\n357\tsjk = τ ⟨µk , ϕqj ⟩, πjk = Softmaxk (sjk )\n358\t(t+1)\n359\t(t) (t) \u0001\n360\t4:\n361\tZjk ← Softmax\n362\t\u0011\n363\t\u0010 P k ξj sjk\n364\t(t+1)\n365\t(t)\n366\t(t+1)\n367\t5:\n368\tξj\n369\t← σ λ1ξ k Zjk log πjk + log 1−b\n370\tb\n371\tP\n372\tP (t+1) (t+1) \u0001\n373\t(t+1)\n374\tZjk ϕqj\n375\t6:\n376\tµk\n377\t← Norm i:ys =k ϕsi + j ξj\n378\ti\n379\t7: end for\n380\t8: Centre all embeddings: ϕ̃ ← ϕ − ϕ̄\n381\t// Phase 2\n382\t9: for t = 0, . . . , Tcal − 1 do\n383\t10:\n384\tljk = τ ⟨ϕ̃qj , µ̃k ⟩; minimise Ltrans w.r.t. {µ̃k }\n385\t11: end for\n386\tP ljk\n387\t1\n388\treturn\n389\t\u0001 ŷj = arg maxk ljk , ôj = σ − log N k e −\n390\tlog b\n391\t\n392\tfor episodic evaluation. Each dataset is divided into 5 folds.\n393\tEpisodes are drawn from the 4 non-held-out folds with the\n394\theld-out fold rotated across iterations.\n395\tESC-50 [21] contains 2,000 five-second environmental\n396\tsound clips across 50 classes (40 clips/class) with 5 built-in\n397\tcross-validation folds. We designate 25 classes (indices 0–24)\n398\tas the inlier pool and the remaining 25 classes (indices 25–49)\n399\tas the outlier pool. Each episode samples 5 inlier and 5 outlier\n400\tclasses from their respective pools.\n401\tFSD-Kaggle2018 [22] contains 9,473 variable-length clips\n402\tacross 41 general-purpose sound-event classes (94–300\n403\tclips/class). Because the dataset has no built-in folds, we\n404\tassign 5 stratified folds deterministically (seed = 0). We use 20\n405\tclasses (indices 0–19) as inlier pool and 21 classes (indices\n406\t20–40) as outlier pool, each episode samples 5 + 5 from these\n407\tpools.\n408\tUrbanSound8K [23] contains 8,732 urban sound clips\n409\tacross 10 classes (374–1,000 clips/class). The original 10 folds\n410\tare remapped to 5 by merging consecutive pairs (folds 1–\n411\t2 → 1, 3–4 → 2, . . . ). With 5 inlier classes (indices 0–4) and 5\n412\toutlier classes (indices 5–9), every episode uses all 10 classes.\n413\tB. Experimental Setup\n414\tEach configuration uses 5-way open-set episodes with 5\n415\tinlier classes and 5 outlier classes drawn from their respective\n416\tpools. We evaluate both 1-shot and 5-shot support samples.\n417\tSupport samples come exclusively from inlier classes. All\n418\tquery samples are unlabeled at test time. Each inlier class contributes 4 query samples, whereas each outlier class contributes\n419\t1, 4, or 16 query samples, yielding 20%, 50%, and 80% outlier\n420\tratios. Under the 1-shot setting, this gives 5 support samples\n421\tper episode. Under 5-shot, 25 support samples per episode.\n422\tQuery set sizes are 25, 40, and 100 for the three outlier ratios,\n423\tregardless of shot count.\n424\t\n425\tWe follow a 5-fold cross-fold protocol with the heldout fold excluded from episode sampling. 300 episodes are\n426\tdrawn per fold, yielding 1,500 episodes per configuration.\n427\tBecause ROLE is a transductive inference method without\n428\tcross-episode training, support and query samples come from\n429\tthe non-held-out folds of the same dataset. Across the full\n430\tbenchmark (8 baselines + ROLE, 3 datasets, 2 shot settings,\n431\t3 outlier ratios), this amounts to 9×3×2×3×1,500 = 243,000\n432\tepisode-level inferences.\n433\tWe report three metrics [25]: inlier classification accuracy\n434\t(Acc), measuring the fraction of correctly classified knownclass query samples, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC), which evaluates the ranking quality\n435\tof the outlier score across all operating thresholds, and area\n436\tunder the precision–recall curve (AUPR), which emphasizes\n437\tdetection performance when the positive (outlier) class is rare\n438\tor dominant depending on the outlier ratio. Table I shows\n439\tper-dataset results across all outlier ratios and shot settings.\n440\tTable II presents the macro-average over all three datasets,\n441\tfurther averaged over the three outlier ratios, under 1-shot and\n442\t5-shot protocols. Both tables arrange methods by row.\n443\tAll methods share the same pre-trained Audio Spectrogram\n444\tTransformer (AST [26], pre-trained on AudioSet [24]). Following standard model-agnostic transductive FSL practice [5],\n445\t[6], the encoder is kept fixed during inference so that the\n446\tcomparison isolates differences in the FOAC algorithm itself.\n447\tWe compare ROLE against four transductive baselines,\n448\tOSTIM [25], OSLO [7], EOL [8], and OPP-T [13], as well as\n449\tfive inductive baselines, OPP-I [13], MET [14], Glocal [15],\n450\tTANE [16], and AISP [17]. All methods use the same frozen\n451\tAST embeddings and identical episodic construction so that\n452\tdifferences can be attributed to the open-set inference rule\n453\trather than to backbone changes. For OPP-I/OPP-T, since the\n454\tAST backbone cannot be retrained, base-class weight vectors\n455\tare computed as the mean of frozen AST embeddings over all\n456\ttraining-fold samples per class, omitting the backbone finetuning stage of the original paper. For MET, Glocal, and\n457\tTANE, all three share the same cosine ProtoNet classifier.\n458\tOnly the outlier scoring function differs, and no per-method\n459\ttemperature tuning is applied, which accounts for their identical Acc values. AISP is evaluated under a class-split protocol\n460\t(60% base-class meta-training / 40% novel-class evaluation).\n461\tUrbanSound8K is excluded for AISP as its 10-class pool yields\n462\tonly 4 novel classes after splitting, which is insufficient for 5way episodes.\n463\tC. Main Results\n464\tTables I and II summarize the benchmark. ROLE achieves\n465\tthe best AUROC in 10 out of the 18 dataset-specific settings.\n466\tAt 20% outlier ratios under 1-shot, OSLO leads in all three\n467\tdatasets: with only a small number of query samples of\n468\tunknown classes, prototype refinement alone suffices and the\n469\tbenefit of decoupled scoring diminishes. Table II confirms\n470\tROLE’s macro-average advantage: ROLE attains 85.88% (1shot) and 92.22% (5-shot) AUROC, improving over MET by\n471\t4.73% and 1.96% respectively, and leads inlier Acc under\n472\t\n473\t\fTABLE I\n474\tD ETAILED PER - DATASET RESULTS . M ETRICS : ACC /AUROC/AUPR (%).\n475\t\n476\tMethod\n477\t\n478\tOutlier\n479\tRatio\n480\t\n481\tESC-50\n482\t5-way 1-shot\n483\t\n484\tFSD-Kaggle2018\n485\t5-way 5-shot\n486\t\n487\t5-way 1-shot\n488\t\n489\tUrbanSound8K\n490\t\n491\t5-way 5-shot\n492\t\n493\t5-way 1-shot\n494\t\n495\t5-way 5-shot\n496\t