ut lacks a decoupled scoring head, so its\n937\tAUROC collapses under 80% outliers (e.g. 31.62% on ESC50 1-shot) where corrupted prototypes directly degrade the\n938\tcoupled softmax. Conversely, EOL applies decoupled scoring\n939\twithout prototype refinement; it excels at high outlier ratios\n940\tyet underperforms at 20% (e.g. 71.52% AUROC on ESC-50\n941\t1-shot) because its support-only prototypes are too coarse for\n942\treliable logit calibration. The inductive methods MET [14],\n943\tGlocal [15], and TANE [16] adopt distinct rejection criteria\n944\tbut share a structural limitation: their rejection boundaries\n945\tare calibrated solely from support examples and remain fixed\n946\tat test time, causing degradation as the episode outlier ratio\n947\tshifts. ROLE sidesteps these failure modes by jointly refining\n948\tprototypes with inlierness gating and adapting rejection to the\n949\tepisode outlier prior, although acoustically similar unknown\n950\tsounds may still influence refinement.\n951\t\n952\t39.03 90.84\n953\t50.07 87.78\n954\t31.62 89.04\n955\t37.47 87.28\n956\t37.68 88.08\n957\t44.53 87.28\n958\t43.18 87.28\n959\t40.98 87.28\n960\t—\n961\t43.87 88.46\n962\t\n963\t74.73\n964\t77.96\n965\t69.08\n966\t61.86\n967\t61.48\n968\t81.79\n969\t74.00\n970\t69.02\n971\t\n972\t45.77\n973\t55.89\n974\t39.98\n975\t38.24\n976\t38.02\n977\t60.13\n978\t50.33\n979\t45.55\n980\t\n981\t82.08\n982\t\n983\t59.85\n984\t\n985\t57.77 88.38\n986\t55.33 86.75\n987\t74.00 88.30\n988\t62.50 87.31\n989\t56.05 85.79\n990\t68.47 87.31\n991\t67.39 87.31\n992\t65.51 87.31\n993\t—\n994\t75.02 88.39\n995\t\n996\t60.81\n997\t66.54\n998\t86.83\n999\t61.99\n1000\t57.33\n1001\t82.16\n1002\t74.38\n1003\t69.34\n1004\t\n1005\t63.26\n1006\t65.04\n1007\t86.57\n1008\t63.76\n1009\t59.18\n1010\t81.07\n1011\t74.21\n1012\t70.21\n1013\t\n1014\t84.85\n1015\t\n1016\t84.54\n1017\t\n1018\t75.88 85.86\n1019\t70.04 72.77\n1020\t92.58 87.45\n1021\t85.78 87.05\n1022\t76.84 70.47\n1023\t88.53 87.05\n1024\t88.14 87.05\n1025\t87.28 87.05\n1026\t—\n1027\t93.45 88.07\n1028\t\n1029\t42.53\n1030\t35.63\n1031\t87.52\n1032\t61.91\n1033\t49.29\n1034\t82.00\n1035\t74.26\n1036\t69.24\n1037\t\n1038\t85.68\n1039\t72.66\n1040\t96.27\n1041\t86.09\n1042\t79.19\n1043\t93.75\n1044\t90.92\n1045\t89.16\n1046\t\n1047\t87.33\n1048\t\n1049\t96.15\n1050\t\n1051\tTABLE II\n1052\tM ACRO - AVERAGED RESULTS OVER ESC-50, FSD-K AGGLE 2018, AND\n1053\tU RBAN S OUND 8K, FURTHER AVERAGED OVER THREE OUTLIER RATIOS\n1054\tPER DATASET. ACC /AUROC/AUPR (%).\n1055\t\n1056\t5-way 1-shot\n1057\t\n1058\tMethod\n1059\t\n1060\t5-way 5-shot\n1061\t\n1062\tAcc AUROC AUPR Acc AUROC AUPR\n1063\tOSTIM [25] 85.91\n1064\tOSLO [7]\n1065\t79.63\n1066\tEOL [8]\n1067\t85.05\n1068\tOPP-I [13]\n1069\t82.12\n1070\tOPP-T [13] 77.62\n1071\tMET [14]\n1072\t82.12\n1073\tGlocal [15]\n1074\t82.12\n1075\tTANE [16]\n1076\t82.12\n1077\tAISP [17]\n1078\t81.07\n1079\tROLE (Ours) 85.19\n1080\t\n1081\t62.70\n1082\t62.59\n1083\t78.85\n1084\t73.89\n1085\t57.88\n1086\t81.15\n1087\t80.40\n1088\t78.40\n1089\t80.47\n1090\t85.88\n1091\t\n1092\t67.84\n1093\t69.72\n1094\t71.73\n1095\t72.12\n1096\t62.81\n1097\t78.89\n1098\t77.88\n1099\t76.04\n1100\t79.40\n1101\t82.29\n1102\t\n1103\t93.45\n1104\t90.34\n1105\t93.28\n1106\t92.90\n1107\t89.52\n1108\t92.90\n1109\t92.90\n1110\t92.90\n1111\t91.88\n1112\t93.49\n1113\t\n1114\t68.74\n1115\t70.75\n1116\t88.55\n1117\t75.90\n1118\t63.66\n1119\t90.26\n1120\t86.76\n1121\t83.64\n1122\t88.19\n1123\t92.22\n1124\t\n1125\t73.26\n1126\t76.82\n1127\t80.51\n1128\t74.41\n1129\t67.21\n1130\t87.90\n1131\t84.37\n1132\t81.60\n1133\t86.11\n1134\t89.50\n1135\t\n1136\tD. Ablation Study\n1137\tTable III evaluates four component variants of ROLE on\n1138\tESC-50 and FSD-Kaggle2018, macro-averaged across three\n1139\toutlier ratios (20%, 50%, 80%) and both shot settings. UrbanSound8K is omitted because its 10 classes make each episode\n1140\tcover all inlier/outlier classes, limiting ablation representative-\n1141\t\n1142\t\fTABLE III\n1143\tA BLATION RESULTS MACRO - AVERAGED OVER ESC-50 AND\n1144\tFSD-K AGGLE 2018, THREE OUTLIER RATIOS , AND BOTH SHOT SETTINGS .\n1145\tF ORMAT: MEAN ± 95 % CI (%).\n1146\t\n1147\tVariant\n1148\tROLE\n1149\tw/o Phase 1\n1150\tw/o ξ-gate (ξj ≡ 1)\n1151\tw/o Phase 2\n1152\tw/o b-prior\n1153\t\n1154\tAUROC\n1155\t\n1156\tAcc\n1157\t\n1158\t93.82 ± 0.16\n1159\t91.91 ± 0.16\n1160\t71.77 ± 0.29\n1161\t92.53 ± 0.17\n1162\t89.83 ± 0.15\n1163\t\n1164\t93.53 ± 0.29\n1165\t91.88 ± 0.28\n1166\t90.01 ± 0.45\n1167\t92.81 ± 0.32\n1168\t90.09 ± 0.30\n1169\t\n1170\tness.\n1171\tThe ablation reveals a clear hierarchy of contributions.\n1172\tRemoving ξ-gating causes by far the largest AUROC drop\n1173\t(−22.05%), confirming that inlierness gating is the most\n1174\tcritical mechanism: without it, Phase 1 propagates outlier\n1175\tcontributions uniformly into prototypes, severely corrupting\n1176\tthe logit geometry. Removing the prior-adaptive shift (w/o bprior) causes the next-largest degradation (−3.99% AUROC,\n1177\t−3.44% Acc), showing that calibrating the inlierness threshold\n1178\tto the episode outlier proportion is essential for both detection\n1179\tand classification. Removing Phase 1 entirely yields moderate drops (−1.91% AUROC, −1.65% Acc), indicating that\n1180\tPhase 2 can partially recover from a coarser initialisation.\n1181\tDisabling Phase 2 causes the smallest AUROC drop (−1.29%)\n1182\tbut reduces Acc by 0.72%, confirming that the transductive\n1183\toptimisation further corrects few-shot class geometry beyond\n1184\twhat Phase 1 alone achieves.\n1185\tIV. C ONCLUSIONS\n1186\tWe presented ROLE, a transductive inference procedure for\n1187\tfew-shot open-set audio classification that couples inliernessguided prototype refinement with a prior-adaptive free-energy\n1188\trejection score and a transductive prototype optimization objective. This design directly addresses the core tension of\n1189\ttransductive FOAC: query set transduction benefits prototype\n1190\testimation only when query samples of unknown classes\n1191\tare identified and suppressed first. Experiments show macroaveraged AUROC/AUPR gains, especially at moderate-to-high\n1192\toutlier ratios; ablations verify that both stages are necessary.\n1193\tAll experiments use a fixed pre-trained AST encoder, and\n1194\tstronger encoder adaptation and inlierness estimation remain\n1195\tdirections for future work.\n1196\tACKNOWLEDGMENT\n1197\tThis work was partly supported by the national natural\n1198\tscience foundation of China (62371195, 62111530145), the\n1199\texchange project of the 10th Meeting of China-Croatia Science\n1200\tand Technology Cooperation Committee (10-34), and the\n1201\tnational undergraduate training program for innovation and\n1202\tentrepreneurship (202510561025 and 202510561026).\n1203\tR EFERENCES\n1204\t[1] Y. Li, J. Li, Y. Si, J. Tan, and Q. He, “Few-shot class-incremental audio\n1205\tclassification with adaptive mitigation of forgetting and overfitting,”\n1206\tTASLP, vol. 32, pp. 2297–2311, 2024.\n1207\t\n1208\t[2] Y. Si, Y. Li, J. Tan, G. Chen, Q. Li, and M. Russo, “Fully few-shot classincremental audio classification with adaptive improvement of stability\n1209\tand plasticity,” TASLP, vol. 33, pp. 418–433, 2025.\n1210\t[3] Y. Li, W. Cao, J. Tan, Q. Li, and G. Chen, “Few-shot classincremental audio classification using pseudo-incrementally trained embedding learner and continually updated stochastic classifier,” TASLP,\n1211\tvol. 33, pp. 3880–3895, 2025.\n1212\t[4] Y. Li, W. Cao, W. Xie, J. Li, and E. Benetos, “Few-shot class-incremental\n1213\taudio classification using dynamically expanded classifier with selfattention modified prototypes,” TMM, vol. 26, pp. 1346–1360, 2024.\n1214\t[5] M. Boudiaf, I. Ziko, J. Rony, J. Dolz, P. Piantanida, and I. Ben Ayed,\n1215\t“Information maximization for few-shot learning,” in NeurIPS, vol. 33,\n1216\t2020, pp. 2445–2457.\n1217\t[6] J. Liu, L. Song, and Y. Qin, “Prototype rectification for few-shot\n1218\tlearning,” in ECCV, 2020, pp. 741–756.\n1219\t[7] M. Boudiaf, E. Bennequin et al., “Open-set likelihood maximization for\n1220\tfew-shot learning,” in CVPR, 2023, pp. 24 007–24 016.\n1221\t[8] M. Ochal, M. Patacchiola, M. Boudiaf, and S. Wang, “Transductive few-shot open-set recognition by enhancing outlier logits,”\n1222\tarXiv:2408.02052, 2024.\n1223\t[9] S. Wu, H. Luo, and X. Lin, “Tnpnet: An approach to few-shot open-set\n1224\trecognition via contextual transductive learning,” Neurocomputing, vol.\n1225\t621, p. 129276, 2025.\n1226\t[10] J. Snell, K. Swersky, and R. S. Zemel, “Prototypical networks for fewshot learning,” in NeurIPS, 2017.\n1227\t[11] C. Finn, P. Abbeel, and S. Levine, “Model-agnostic meta-learning for\n1228\tfast adaptation of deep networks,” in ICML, 2017, pp. 1126–1135.\n1229\t[12] Y. Tian, Y. Wang, D. Krishnan, J. B. Tenenbaum, and P. Isola, “Rethinking few-shot image classification: A good embedding is all you need?”\n1230\tin ECCV, 2020, pp. 266–282.\n1231\t[13] L.-Y. Sun and W.-T. Chu, “Overall positive prototype for few-shot openset recognition,” Pattern Recognition, vol. 151, p. 110400, 2024.\n1232\t[14] H. Sapkota, K. P. Neupane, and Q. Yu, “Meta evidential transformer for\n1233\tfew-shot open-set recognition,” in ICML, 2024, pp. 43 389–43 406.\n1234\t[15] H. Wang, G. Pang, P. Wang, L. Zhang, W. Wei, and Y. Zhang, “Glocal\n1235\tenergy-based learning for few-shot open-set recognition,” in CVPR,\n1236\t2023, pp. 7507–7516.\n1237\t[16] S. Huang, J. Ma, G. Han, and S.-F. Chang, “Task-adaptive negative\n1238\tenvision for few-shot open-set recognition,” in CVPR, 2022, pp. 7161–\n1239\t7170.\n1240\t[17] Y. Li, J. Tan, Q. Li, G. Chen, S. Huang, and T. Virtanen, “Few-shot openset audio classification using attention information-fused prototypes,”\n1241\tTASLP, vol. 34, pp. 1929–1943, 2026.\n1242\t[18] L.-Y. Li, T.-H. Lo, J.-W. Hung, S.-C. Huang, and B. Chen, “Few-shot\n1243\topen-set keyword spotting with multi-stage training,” in APSIPA ASC,\n1244\t2024, pp. 1–5.\n1245\t[19] M. Rusci and T. Tuytelaars, “Few-shot open-set learning for on-device\n1246\tcustomization of keyword spotting systems,” in Interspeech, 2023, pp.\n1247\t2768–2772.\n1248\t[20] M. Yang, Q. He, J. Huang, Y. Chen, Y. Li, and Z. Liu, “Crossdomain few-shot open-set keyword spotting using keyword adaptation\n1249\tand prototype reprojection,” in ICASSP, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n1250\t[21] K. J. Piczak, “Esc: Dataset for environmental sound classification,” in\n1251\tACM Multimedia, 2015, pp. 1015–1018.\n1252\t[22] E. Fonseca, M. Plakal, F. Font, D. P. W. Ellis, X. Favory, J. Pons, and\n1253\tX. Serra, “General-purpose tagging of freesound audio with audioset\n1254\tlabels,” in DCASE Workshop, 2018, pp. 69–73.\n1255\t[23] J. Salamon, C. Jacoby, and J. P. Bello, “A dataset and taxonomy for\n1256\turban sound research,” in ACM Multimedia, 2014, pp. 1041–1044.\n1257\t[24] J. F. Gemmeke, D. P. W. Ellis, D. Freedman et al., “Audio set: An\n1258\tontology and human-labeled dataset for audio events,” in ICASSP, 2017,\n1259\tpp. 776–780.\n1260\t[25] M. Boudiaf, E. Bennequin, M. Tami, C. Hudelot, A. Toubhans, P. Piantanida, and I. Ben Ayed, “Model-agnostic few-shot open-set recognition,” arXiv:2206.09236, 2022.\n1261\t[26] Y. Gong, Y.-A. Chung, and J. Glass, “Ast: Audio spectrogram transformer,” in Interspeech, 2021, pp. 571–575.\n1262\t\n1263\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "47321685-9a74-4a36-b59e-a41061859b68", "uuid": "852bfe6f-83cd-4b6d-9783-4a88f8c5cc9f", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T07:41:27.367Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607_26607.txt", "content": "arXiv:2607.26607v1 [cs.SD] 29 Jul 2026\n\nFew-Shot Open-Set Audio Classification via\nTransductive Prototype Refinement and Class Logit\nEnhancement\nTianyan Deng\n\nYanxiong Li*\n\nSchool of Mathematics\nSouth China University of Technology\nGuangzhou, China\n202330400581@mail.scut.edu.cn\n\nSchool of Electronic and Information Engineering\nSouth China University of Technology\nGuangzhou, China\neeyxli@scut.edu.cn\n\nRui Gao\n\nJiahao Du\n\nSchool of Electronic and Information Engineering\nSouth China University of Technology\nGuangzhou, China\n202330350401@mail.scut.edu.cn\n\nSchool of Electronic and Information Engineering\nSouth China University of Technology\nGuangzhou, China\n202330350311@mail.scut.edu.cn\n\nAbstract—Few-shot Open-set audio classification requires classifying query samples from known classes with a few labeled\nsupport samples while rejecting query samples from unknown\nclasses. Transductive inference jointly observes the full unlabeled query set to improve prototype estimation, yet standard\ntransductive updates do not distinguish known from unknown\nquery samples, leaving prototypes vulnerable to open-set contamination. Drawing on latent-inlierness weighting and decoupled\nscoring for unknown-class samples, we propose a two-phase\ntransductive method operating over a frozen audio encoder. First,\neach query sample is assigned a latent inlierness score that\ndown-weights likely unknown-class samples, so that prototype\nrefinement is driven primarily by known-class evidence. The\nrefined prototypes are then directly optimized on a transductive\nloss combining support cross-entropy, inlierness-weighted conditional entropy minimization, and inlierness-weighted marginal\nentropy maximization, while open-set rejection uses a prioradaptive free-energy score that adjusts its threshold with the\nprior proportion of unknown-class samples, decoupling detection\nfrom classification. Experiments on three audio datasets show\nour method achieves state-of-the-art results for few-shot openset audio classification under multiple experimental conditions.\nCode is available at https://github.com/Gostyan/ROLE\nIndex Terms—Few-shot open-set classification, transductive\ninference, audio classification\n\nI. I NTRODUCTION\nReal-world few-shot audio classification [1]–[4] rarely satisfies the closed-set assumption: query samples routinely include\nsounds outside classes of support set. In the transductive\nsetting, a model observes the full unlabeled query set jointly\nduring inference, which improves episode-level prototype estimation [5], [6]. However, standard transductive updates treat\nall query samples equally and cannot distinguish known-class\nfrom unknown-class samples, so prototype estimation degrade\n∗ Corresponding author: Yanxiong Li (eeyxli@scut.edu.cn).\n\nas the unknown-class proportion grows [7]–[9]. Existing transductive few-shot methods mainly target closed-set accuracy,\nwhile dedicated open-set methods do not fully exploit query\nset structure. This tension is especially pronounced in audio,\nwhere environmental sounds span an open-ended category\nspace and deployment conditions are highly variable.\nFew-shot learning provides metric- and meta-learning foundations via Prototypical Networks [10], MAML [11], and\nembedding baselines [12]. Few-shot open-set methods such as\nOPP [13], MET [14], Glocal [15], TANE [16], and AISP [17]\nimprove inductive rejection through various prototype and\nscoring strategies. Transductive methods, such as TIM [5] and\nPrototype Rectification [6], improve closed-set class estimation but are not designed for open-set episodes. Contextual\ntransductive FSOR [9] is the closest prior transductive open-set\nextension. OSLO [7] introduces latent-inlierness weighting for\nprototype estimation and EOL [8] proposes decoupled outlierlogit scoring. Few-shot open-set keyword-spotting methods\ntackle open-set challenges through multi-stage training [18],\non-device calibration [19], and cross-domain reprojection [20],\nbut remain task-specific and do not address transductive prototype contamination for general audio classification.\nWe propose Refinement-based Outlier-Logit Enhancement\n(ROLE), a transductive algorithm for Few-shot Open-set Audio Classification (FOAC) operating on a frozen audio encoder.\nOur contributions are as follows.\nWe propose ROLE, a unified transductive inference procedure that couples latent-inlierness-guided prototype refinement with a prior-adaptive free-energy rejection score\nin a single episode-level inference rule, requiring only a\nfrozen pre-trained encoder with no episodic meta-training\nor backbone fine-tuning.\n• Evaluated on ESC-50, FSD-Kaggle2018, and Urban•\n\n\fSound8K, under 5-way 1-shot/5-shot and 20%/50%/80%\noutlier ratios, our method achieves the highest AUROC\nscores in 10 out of 18 evaluation settings, with macromean AUROC of 85.88/92.22 vs. 81.15/90.26 for the\nstrongest baseline MET.\nII. M ETHOD\nA. Problem Formulation\nWe consider a transductive N -way, K-shot open-set\ns\nepisode. The support set S = {(xsi , ysi )}N\ni=1 (Ns = N K)\ncontains labeled examples from N novel classes, and the\nNq\nmay contain both knownunlabeled query set Q = {xqj }j=1\nclass samples (inliers) and unknown-class samples (outliers).\nThe full query set is observed jointly during inference. The\ngoal is to classify inlier query samples into one of N support\nclasses and reject outliers with a scalar outlier score. We write\n[N ] = {1, . . . , N } for the inlier label set.\nROLE operates on a frozen pre-trained audio encoder\nf (·) that maps each waveform to a normalized embedding\nϕ = f (x)/∥f (x)∥. We write ϕsi and ϕqj for support and\nquery embeddings respectively. ROLE optimizes only episodespecific parameters: class prototypes {µk }N\nk=1 , re-initialized\nper episode.\nB. Design Principles\nROLE is structured as a two-phase pipeline, and each\nphase addresses a distinct challenge in transductive open-set\nclassification.\nPrototype bias from undifferentiated transduction. Standard transductive updates (e.g., TIM [5], Prototype Rectification [6]) incorporate all query evidence equally into prototype\nestimation. In open-set episodes, unknown-class query samples\ninevitably participate in these updates, pulling prototypes away\nfrom the true known-class centers and the bias grows with the\nunknown-class ratio. Phase 1 mitigates this by introducing a\nper-query latent inlierness score: query samples assessed as\nunlikely to belong to any known class are automatically downweighted, so that primarily known-class evidence refines the\nprototypes.\nConflated classification and rejection under a single\nsoftmax. A natural approach uses the softmax posterior over\nknown classes for both classification and open-set rejection.\nHowever, softmax normalizes only over known classes and\ncannot express “this query sample belongs to none of them”:\nan unknown-class query sample equidistant from all prototypes\nreceives a uniform but non-negligible posterior, appearing\nsimilar to a genuinely ambiguous inlier. Phase 2 decouples\nthe two objectives by deriving classification from a class-wise\nsoftmax and open-set rejection from a sigmoid on the negative\nlog-mean-exp of the same logits, which explicitly measures\nwhether total class evidence justifies considering the query\nsample as known-class.\nFigure 1 summarizes the resulting pipeline.\n\nC. Unified Transductive Inference\nGiven a labeled support set and an unlabeled open-set query\nset, ROLE first refines prototypes under latent inlierness and\nthen further optimizes them on a transductive loss with a prioradaptive decoupled scoring objective.\nPhase 1: Inlierness-guided prototype refinement. Phase 1\nrefines class prototypes using query-batch evidence while\npreventing unknown-class query samples from biasing the\nresult. We write Norm(·) = (·)/∥ · ∥ for ℓ2 normaliza(0)\ntion. Prototypes\nare initialized from support means: µk =\nP\nNorm( i:ys =k ϕsi ). Each query sample j carries a latent\ni\n\n(0)\n\ninlierness score ξj ∈ (0, 1) (initialized to ξj = 1), representing the model’s belief that query sample j belongs to\nsome known class. Following the block-coordinate descent\nof OSLO [7], three variables—soft assignments Z, inlierness\nξ, and prototypes µ—are updated alternately for Tboot iterations with support assignments held fixed. At iteration t,\n(t)\n(t)\nlet sjk = τ ⟨µk , ϕqj ⟩ denote the temperature-scaled cosine\nsimilarity between query sample j and prototype k, and\n(t)\n(t)\nπjk = Softmaxk∈[N ] (sjk ) the posterior probability that query\nsample j belongs to class k.\nSoft assignments are modulated by inlierness rather than\ncommitted to hard pseudo-labels, which would propagate\nassignment errors directly into prototypes:\n\u0010\n\u0011\n(t+1)\n(t) (t)\nZjk\n= Softmaxk∈[N ] ξj sjk\n(1)\nHere ξj acts as an inverse temperature: when ξj ≈ 1 the assignment peaks at the nearest prototype, when ξj ≈ 0 it flattens\nto uniform, neutralizing the query sample’s contribution. The\ninlierness score is then updated via the expected log-posterior\nunder the soft assignment, measuring how well the knownclass structure explains query sample j:\n!\n1−b\n1 X (t+1)\n(t)\n(t+1)\nZjk log πjk + log\n(2)\nξj\n= Sigmoid\nλξ\nb\nk\n\nwhere λξ > 0 controls sensitivity and b is the prior outlier\nproportion of the episode. The bias term log 1−b\nshifts ξj\nb\ndownward when b is large: at high outlier ratios, the default\nσ(0) = 0.5 would place many outliers at an ambiguous midpoint, making them hard to suppress, the prior shift pushes the\nthreshold so that only query samples with strong known-class\nevidence retain high inlierness. Prototypes are re-estimated\nby combining fixed support geometry with inlierness-filtered\nquery evidence:\n\n\nNq\nX\nX\n(t+1)\n(t+1) (t+1)\nµk\n= Norm\nϕs i +\nξj\nZjk ϕqj  (3)\ni: ysi =k\n\nj=1\n\nThe product ξj Zjk provides double gating: only query samples\nthat are both likely inliers and confidently assigned to class k\ninfluence µk , preventing unknown-class query samples from\nshifting prototypes.\nPhase 2: Transductive prototype optimization with decoupled scoring. Phase 2 directly optimizes the class pro-\n\n\fFig. 1. Overview of ROLE. A frozen audio encoder maps support and query audio to normalized embeddings. Phase 1 performs inlierness-guided prototype\nrefinement. Phase 2 centers episode geometry, optimizes prototypes with ξ-weighted transductive losses, and uses prior-adaptive free-energy rejection decoupled\nfrom classification.\n\ntotypes {µk } on the episode-level transductive loss, further adapting the refined geometry to the observed query\ndistribution. Because frozen encoders carry a shared bias\nfrom pre-training,\nP\nPwe first subtract the episode mean ϕ̄ =\n1\n(\nϕ\n+\ns\ni\ni\nj ϕqj ), yielding centered prototypes µ̃k =\nNs +Nq\n(T\n\non a transductive loss:\nN\n\ntrans\n\nL\n\n)\n\nµk boot − ϕ̄ and centered query samples ϕ̃qj = ϕqj − ϕ̄.\nThe logit for query sample j on class k is ljk = τ ⟨ϕ̃qj , µ̃k ⟩,\nthe temperature-scaled cosine similarity between the centered\nquery sample and prototype, where τ > 0 is a fixed temperature shared across both phases.\nFollowing EOL [8], classification and rejection are decoupled rather than sharing a single softmax:\n\nôj = Sigmoid − log N1\n\nN\nX\nk=1\n\n!\neljk − log b ,\n\ns\n1 X\n= −\nlog pi,ysi + λq\nNs i=1\nX\n+ λma\np̂k log p̂k\n\nP\n\nj ξj H(pj )\n\nP\n\nj ξj\n\n(5)\n\nk\n\n\u0001\nwhere p̂k =\nj ξj pjk\nj ξj is the ξ-weighted marginal\nclass distribution; λq and λma > 0 are hyperparameters\nbalancing the three objectives. The three terms are as follows.\nThe support cross-entropy anchors prototype updates to labeled data. The ξj -weighted conditional entropy minimization\nencourages sharp per-query predictions for likely inliers while\nignoring outliers (ξj ≈ 0), where the per-query entropy is\nX\nH(pj ) = −\npjk log pjk .\n(6)\nP\n\n\u0001\u000e P\n\nk\n\n(4)\n\npjk = Softmaxk∈[N ] (ljk ).\n\nThe class posterior pjk gives the predicted class ŷj =\narg maxk ljk . The outlier score ôj is derived from the negative\nlog-mean-exp\nP of the logits: when no class logit is\nP high,\nthe sum k eljk is small relative to N , so − log N1 k eljk\nis large and ôj is correspondingly large. The prior-adaptive\nshift − log b further lowers the free-energy threshold required\nfor inlier acceptance as the outlier proportion b grows, so\nthat the boundary between inlier and outlier decisions adapts\nautomatically to episode difficulty.\nThe prototypes {µ̃k } are optimized over Tcal gradient steps\n\nThe ξ-weighted marginal entropyPmaximization: since\np̂k log p̂k ≤ 0, minimizing +λma k p̂k log p̂k pushes p̂\ntoward the uniform distribution, encouraging the prototype\ngeometry to assign inlier query samples evenly across known\nclasses and preventing collapse to a dominant class.\nUnlike OSLO/EOL, ROLE uses inlierness-gated TIM-style\noptimization before prior-adaptive free-energy scoring.\nAlgorithm 1 summarizes the complete procedure of ROLE.\nIII. E XPERIMENTS\nA. Datasets\nWe evaluate different methods on three audio datasets: ESC50 [21], FSD-Kaggle2018 [22] and UrbanSound8K [23]. The\nencoder is pre-trained on AudioSet [24] and kept frozen.\nNo dataset contributes a training split. All clips are used\n\n\fAlgorithm 1 ROLE\nRequire: Support set S, query set Q, frozen encoder f\nEnsure: Predicted label ŷj and outlier score ôj for each query\nsample j\n(0)\n1: Encode and ℓ2 -normalise all embeddings; init µk from\n(0)\nsupport means, ξj =1\n2: for t = 0, . . . , Tboot − 1 do\n// Phase 1\n(t)\n(t)\n(t)\n(t)\n3:\nsjk = τ ⟨µk , ϕqj ⟩, πjk = Softmaxk (sjk )\n(t+1)\n(t) (t) \u0001\n4:\nZjk ← Softmax\n\u0011\n\u0010 P k ξj sjk\n(t+1)\n(t)\n(t+1)\n5:\nξj\n← σ λ1ξ k Zjk log πjk + log 1−b\nb\nP\nP (t+1) (t+1) \u0001\n(t+1)\nZjk ϕqj\n6:\nµk\n← Norm i:ys =k ϕsi + j ξj\ni\n7: end for\n8: Centre all embeddings: ϕ̃ ← ϕ − ϕ̄\n// Phase 2\n9: for t = 0, . . . , Tcal − 1 do\n10:\nljk = τ ⟨ϕ̃qj , µ̃k ⟩; minimise Ltrans w.r.t. {µ̃k }\n11: end for\nP ljk\n1\nreturn\n\u0001 ŷj = arg maxk ljk , ôj = σ − log N k e −\nlog b\n\nfor episodic evaluation. Each dataset is divided into 5 folds.\nEpisodes are drawn from the 4 non-held-out folds with the\nheld-out fold rotated across iterations.\nESC-50 [21] contains 2,000 five-second environmental\nsound clips across 50 classes (40 clips/class) with 5 built-in\ncross-validation folds. We designate 25 classes (indices 0–24)\nas the inlier pool and the remaining 25 classes (indices 25–49)\nas the outlier pool. Each episode samples 5 inlier and 5 outlier\nclasses from their respective pools.\nFSD-Kaggle2018 [22] contains 9,473 variable-length clips\nacross 41 general-purpose sound-event classes (94–300\nclips/class). Because the dataset has no built-in folds, we\nassign 5 stratified folds deterministically (seed = 0). We use 20\nclasses (indices 0–19) as inlier pool and 21 classes (indices\n20–40) as outlier pool, each episode samples 5 + 5 from these\npools.\nUrbanSound8K [23] contains 8,732 urban sound clips\nacross 10 classes (374–1,000 clips/class). The original 10 folds\nare remapped to 5 by merging consecutive pairs (folds 1–\n2 → 1, 3–4 → 2, . . . ). With 5 inlier classes (indices 0–4) and 5\noutlier classes (indices 5–9), every episode uses all 10 classes.\nB. Experimental Setup\nEach configuration uses 5-way open-set episodes with 5\ninlier classes and 5 outlier classes drawn from their respective\npools. We evaluate both 1-shot and 5-shot support samples.\nSupport samples come exclusively from inlier classes. All\nquery samples are unlabeled at test time. Each inlier class contributes 4 query samples, whereas each outlier class contributes\n1, 4, or 16 query samples, yielding 20%, 50%, and 80% outlier\nratios. Under the 1-shot setting, this gives 5 support samples\nper episode. Under 5-shot, 25 support samples per episode.\nQuery set sizes are 25, 40, and 100 for the three outlier ratios,\nregardless of shot count.\n\nWe follow a 5-fold cross-fold protocol with the heldout fold excluded from episode sampling. 300 episodes are\ndrawn per fold, yielding 1,500 episodes per configuration.\nBecause ROLE is a transductive inference method without\ncross-episode training, support and query samples come from\nthe non-held-out folds of the same dataset. Across the full\nbenchmark (8 baselines + ROLE, 3 datasets, 2 shot settings,\n3 outlier ratios), this amounts to 9×3×2×3×1,500 = 243,000\nepisode-level inferences.\nWe report three metrics [25]: inlier classification accuracy\n(Acc), measuring the fraction of correctly classified knownclass query samples, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC), which evaluates the ranking quality\nof the outlier score across all operating thresholds, and area\nunder the precision–recall curve (AUPR), which emphasizes\ndetection performance when the positive (outlier) class is rare\nor dominant depending on the outlier ratio. Table I shows\nper-dataset results across all outlier ratios and shot settings.\nTable II presents the macro-average over all three datasets,\nfurther averaged over the three outlier ratios, under 1-shot and\n5-shot protocols. Both tables arrange methods by row.\nAll methods share the same pre-trained Audio Spectrogram\nTransformer (AST [26], pre-trained on AudioSet [24]). Following standard model-agnostic transductive FSL practice [5],\n[6], the encoder is kept fixed during inference so that the\ncomparison isolates differences in the FOAC algorithm itself.\nWe compare ROLE against four transductive baselines,\nOSTIM [25], OSLO [7], EOL [8], and OPP-T [13], as well as\nfive inductive baselines, OPP-I [13], MET [14], Glocal [15],\nTANE [16], and AISP [17]. All methods use the same frozen\nAST embeddings and identical episodic construction so that\ndifferences can be attributed to the open-set inference rule\nrather than to backbone changes. For OPP-I/OPP-T, since the\nAST backbone cannot be retrained, base-class weight vectors\nare computed as the mean of frozen AST embeddings over all\ntraining-fold samples per class, omitting the backbone finetuning stage of the original paper. For MET, Glocal, and\nTANE, all three share the same cosine ProtoNet classifier.\nOnly the outlier scoring function differs, and no per-method\ntemperature tuning is applied, which accounts for their identical Acc values. AISP is evaluated under a class-split protocol\n(60% base-class meta-training / 40% novel-class evaluation).\nUrbanSound8K is excluded for AISP as its 10-class pool yields\nonly 4 novel classes after splitting, which is insufficient for 5way episodes.\nC. Main Results\nTables I and II summarize the benchmark. ROLE achieves\nthe best AUROC in 10 out of the 18 dataset-specific settings.\nAt 20% outlier ratios under 1-shot, OSLO leads in all three\ndatasets: with only a small number of query samples of\nunknown classes, prototype refinement alone suffices and the\nbenefit of decoupled scoring diminishes. Table II confirms\nROLE’s macro-average advantage: ROLE attains 85.88% (1shot) and 92.22% (5-shot) AUROC, improving over MET by\n4.73% and 1.96% respectively, and leads inlier Acc under\n\n\fTABLE I\nD ETAILED PER - DATASET RESULTS . M ETRICS : ACC /AUROC/AUPR (%).\n\nMethod\n\nOutlier\nRatio\n\nESC-50\n5-way 1-shot\n\nFSD-Kaggle2018\n5-way 5-shot\n\n5-way 1-shot\n\nUrbanSound8K\n\n5-way 5-shot\n\n5-way 1-shot\n\n5-way 5-shot\n\nAcc AUROC AUPR Acc AUROC AUPR Acc AUROC AUPR Acc AUROC AUPR Acc AUROC AUPR Acc AUROC AUPR\nOSTIM [25]\nOSLO [7]\nEOL [8]\nOPP-I [13]\nOPP-T [13]\n20%\nMET [14]\nGlocal [15]\nTANE [16]\nAISP [17]\nROLE (Ours)\n\n98.41\n97.54\n96.10\n96.32\n98.23\n96.32\n96.32\n96.32\n93.74\n97.94\n\n94.44\n97.98\n71.52\n88.68\n88.49\n94.49\n94.66\n93.51\n87.14\n96.09\n\n82.81 99.46\n94.17 99.27\n38.11 98.44\n73.24 99.20\n73.64 99.30\n85.85 99.20\n85.42 99.20\n82.44 99.20\n75.34 96.21\n88.99 99.34\n\n96.2